4. What’s the difference between Optional.of(), Optional.ofNullable(), and Optional.empty()?

Optional.of(value) → throws NullPointerException if value is null.

Optional.ofNullable(value) → allows null and returns Optional.empty() if null.

Optional.empty() → explicit empty instance.

Optional<String> name = Optional.ofNullable(getName());
name.ifPresentOrElse(System.out::println, () -> System.out.println("No name"));


Lead insight: Use Optional to avoid null-checks, 
but don’t use it in entity fields or method parameters—only for return types.

